abstract: >-
  The experimental study of fear learning and memory through
  fear conditioning paradigms has provided critical insights
  into the mechanisms underlying threat processing, and the
  etiology of anxiety- and stress-related disorders.
  Reliable estimation of effects and generalizability are
  limited by small sample sizes, in combination with
  substantial interindividual differences, and
  methodological heterogeneity. To overcome these
  challenges, we introduce the “fearbase” - a collaborative,
  harmonized, and continuously growing database that
  aggregates individual participant data from human fear
  conditioning experiments. This resource enables
  well-powered hypothesis-driven and exploratory analyses,
  as well as mega- and meta-analytic research. Here, we
  outline the fearbase content, data access procedures, and
  consortium structure, and invite researchers to join this
  effort. As a continuously expanding resource, the fearbase
  will evolve to incorporate additional measures and
  experimental designs relevant to fear learning and related
  processes. The fearbase aims to accelerate cumulative
  science and serves as a blueprint for collaborative
  data-sharing initiatives in related fields.
keywords:
  - human fear conditioning
  - fear learning
